{:edb/db-before-t 869 :edb/db-after-t 870 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534182 50 #inst "2026-10-06T08:29:46.877Z" 13194139534182 true] [17592186166583 1100 "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2$?" 13194139534182 false] [17592186166583 1100 "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2?$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186166583 1145 "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}\n$$\n\nwhich has order $3$.\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3$." 13194139534182 false] [17592186166583 1145 "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3},$ which has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3.$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186166584 1100 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186166584 1100 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n2. $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" 13194139534182 false] [17592186166584 1145 "A first-order equation is autonomous if it can be written as $y'=f(y)$ with no explicit dependence on $x$.\n\n- I is $y'=y^3$, so it is autonomous.\n- II rearranges to $y'=(y^3+2)/x$ for $x\\ne0$. Its right-hand side explicitly depends on $x$, so it is not autonomous.\n- III rearranges to $y'=1+3\\sqrt y$, which depends only on $y$, so it is autonomous.\n\nThe correct answer is **I and III only**." 13194139534182 false] [17592186166584 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, $y'=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)= y^3.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)= \\dfrac{y^3+2}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)= 1+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534182 true] [17592186166585 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\n- $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\n- $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186166585 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n1. $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n2. $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n3. $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" 13194139534182 false] [17592186166585 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6$.\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e$.\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2$. This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534182 false] [17592186166585 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534182 true] [17592186166745 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280984 13194139534182 true] [17592186166745 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280991 13194139534182 true] [17592186166745 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281033 13194139534182 true] [17592186166745 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281040 13194139534182 true] [17592186166746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281019 13194139534182 true] [17592186166746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281026 13194139534182 true] [17592186166746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281047 13194139534182 true] [17592186166746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281054 13194139534182 true] [17592186166746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281061 13194139534182 true] [17592186166747 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280998 13194139534182 true] [17592186166747 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281005 13194139534182 true] [17592186166747 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281012 13194139534182 true] [17592186167377 1100 "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + x^{3}y = 5y^{4}\\frac{dy}{dx}$?" 13194139534182 false] [17592186167377 1100 "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{3}y=5{y}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}?$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186167377 1145 "The highest derivative appearing is $d^2y/dx^2$. The powers $x^3$ and $y^4$ do not determine the order. Therefore the ODE has order **2**." 13194139534182 false] [17592186167377 1145 "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ which has order $2.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $2.$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186167380 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $2x{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=1$\n- ${y}^{′}-2xy={e}^{y}$\n- $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186167380 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n1. $2xy^{\\prime } = y, y(0) = 1$\n2. $y^{\\prime } - 2xy = e^{y}$\n3. $2xy^{\\prime \\prime } = y^{\\prime }, y(1) = 1, y^{\\prime }(1) = 1$" 13194139534182 false] [17592186167380 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2x{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534182 true] [17592186167380 1145 "System I combines a first-order equation with a specified value $y(0)=1$, so it has the form of a first-order IVP. II has no initial condition. III contains $y''$ and is second order. Select **I only**.\n\nThe classification does not assert existence: system I has no differentiable solution satisfying the equation at $x=0$, because substituting its initial data gives $0=1$." 13194139534182 false] [17592186167437 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′},\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\n- $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=2$\n- $4{y}^{′}-\\frac{1}{3}xy=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186167437 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n1. $\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}y^{\\prime \\prime } = 3y^{\\prime }, y(1) = 2, y^{\\prime }(1) = 1$\n2. $2x^{2}y^{\\prime } = y, y(0) = 2$\n3. $4y^{\\prime } - \\frac{1}{3}xy = \\ln y$" 13194139534182 false] [17592186167437 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534182 true] [17592186167437 1145 "System I is second order because it contains $y''$. System II is a first-order equation with one initial condition. System III has no initial condition. Thus **II only** has the form of a first-order IVP.\n\nSystem II is inconsistent at its initial point: $2(0)^2y'(0)=y(0)$ would require $0=2$. An IVP may be posed without having a solution." 13194139534182 false] [17592186280984 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280985 13194139534182 true] [17592186280984 1089 "q-118779" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280984 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534182 true] [17592186280984 1100 "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}={x}^{8}?$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280984 1113 #uuid "a933fd6d-6a35-5eb0-9d87-5a41bdcc4a21" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280984 1145 "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}},$ which has order $6.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $6.$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280986 13194139534182 true] [17592186280985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280987 13194139534182 true] [17592186280985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280988 13194139534182 true] [17592186280985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280989 13194139534182 true] [17592186280985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280990 13194139534182 true] [17592186280985 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534182 true] [17592186280985 1041 "selection" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280985 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280989 13194139534182 true] [17592186280985 1110 #uuid "ac17ce1a-7905-54b1-913b-6084706ce9ab" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280986 1034 "3" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280986 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534182 true] [17592186280986 1111 #uuid "039b1404-c3c5-5f0d-9a9a-7558551fd64a" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280987 1034 "8" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280987 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534182 true] [17592186280987 1111 #uuid "c34dde87-5094-5eee-91dc-6aa1b724f521" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280988 1034 "7" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280988 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534182 true] [17592186280988 1111 #uuid "f6231a12-ab5e-5bbd-b99c-a535dd85e364" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280989 1034 "6" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280989 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534182 true] [17592186280989 1111 #uuid "2adaaddc-1b23-5ecc-bb68-50ad35a9cc37" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280990 1034 "4" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280990 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534182 true] [17592186280990 1111 #uuid "4f5b2501-b5d5-505e-b2bc-8081f1f55e6f" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280991 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280992 13194139534182 true] [17592186280991 1089 "q-118781" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280991 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534182 true] [17592186280991 1100 "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-{y}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}={y}^{5}?$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280991 1113 #uuid "84db76b0-471c-51dc-bdd6-2d39dffa4f28" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280991 1145 "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280993 13194139534182 true] [17592186280992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280994 13194139534182 true] [17592186280992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280995 13194139534182 true] [17592186280992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280996 13194139534182 true] [17592186280992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280997 13194139534182 true] [17592186280992 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534182 true] [17592186280992 1041 "selection" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280992 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280996 13194139534182 true] [17592186280992 1110 #uuid "d0d987eb-903e-5449-b204-80f83227cb1d" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280993 1034 "3" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280993 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534182 true] [17592186280993 1111 #uuid "28649c78-5975-5e5b-9f64-24e6e19800cb" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280994 1034 "2" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280994 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534182 true] [17592186280994 1111 #uuid "e3c06e4d-4cfb-5018-9420-a1c73a3667b4" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280995 1034 "6" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280995 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534182 true] [17592186280995 1111 #uuid "9f211831-069a-570b-83ed-b75cee7516da" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280996 1034 "4" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280996 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534182 true] [17592186280996 1111 #uuid "66d25487-68f6-5014-8db4-e0cb08a0de0d" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280997 1034 "5" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280997 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534182 true] [17592186280997 1111 #uuid "b29801d2-6483-514e-9099-aa269d9816f5" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280998 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280999 13194139534182 true] [17592186280998 1089 "q-118787" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280998 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534182 true] [17592186280998 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-x=-7y,\\,y(2)=3$\n- ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=0$\n- $x{y}^{″}-6y=14,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280998 1113 #uuid "9102c868-96ec-5673-8dcc-be6cc7ee008d" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280998 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-x=-7y$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,$ and ${y}^{″}(0)=0.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $x{y}^{″}-6y=14$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281000 13194139534182 true] [17592186280999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281001 13194139534182 true] [17592186280999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281002 13194139534182 true] [17592186280999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281003 13194139534182 true] [17592186280999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281004 13194139534182 true] [17592186280999 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534182 true] [17592186280999 1041 "selection" 13194139534182 true] [17592186280999 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281001 13194139534182 true] [17592186280999 1110 #uuid "f458c836-e756-5985-b647-d7229c4f9fa0" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281000 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281000 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281000 1111 #uuid "84525e64-3770-5710-800d-17ca14cb1df0" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281001 1034 "I only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281001 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281001 1111 #uuid "e51c41a2-5a38-50c1-b64e-c02234a5c23a" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281002 1034 "III only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281002 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281002 1111 #uuid "511a2a26-f405-5752-9647-cc01b576f3e1" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281003 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281003 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281003 1111 #uuid "08307e15-cafd-5fa9-b4ce-b10cd892d7a2" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281004 1034 "II only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281004 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281004 1111 #uuid "24974ecf-0ec7-5319-af8e-c5ecd324b478" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281005 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281006 13194139534182 true] [17592186281005 1089 "q-118790" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281005 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534182 true] [17592186281005 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-9x,\\,y(1)=-4$\n- ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y,\\,y(0)=-3$\n- ${y}^{″}+y=3$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281005 1113 #uuid "4db518e4-03ec-5f0b-bee4-e745f4578bc7" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281005 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}=-9x$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=-4.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=-3.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281007 13194139534182 true] [17592186281006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281008 13194139534182 true] [17592186281006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281009 13194139534182 true] [17592186281006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281010 13194139534182 true] [17592186281006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281011 13194139534182 true] [17592186281006 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534182 true] [17592186281006 1041 "selection" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281006 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281010 13194139534182 true] [17592186281006 1110 #uuid "866fad75-1b84-5f9d-b6e3-33fb6596644c" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281007 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281007 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281007 1111 #uuid "905c956b-71ca-53d4-96fe-817ce32a0fee" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281008 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281008 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281008 1111 #uuid "3add1308-38cf-5b33-b911-8b52a18a6451" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281009 1034 "I only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281009 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281009 1111 #uuid "5d533b47-95eb-5b1a-92f1-d16bec403fda" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281010 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281010 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281010 1111 #uuid "66878d39-e481-5c06-bc31-bdbfe95200e9" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281011 1034 "II only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281011 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281011 1111 #uuid "7f87b4c2-55f6-5a21-b85e-5eacbf4399f1" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281012 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281013 13194139534182 true] [17592186281012 1089 "q-118791" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281012 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534182 true] [17592186281012 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x},\\,y(1)=6$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(4)=-3$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281012 1113 #uuid "90a18919-f815-5b77-9ac2-909415c66f7d" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281012 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281013 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281014 13194139534182 true] [17592186281013 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281015 13194139534182 true] [17592186281013 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281016 13194139534182 true] [17592186281013 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281017 13194139534182 true] [17592186281013 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281018 13194139534182 true] [17592186281013 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534182 true] [17592186281013 1041 "selection" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281013 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281015 13194139534182 true] [17592186281013 1110 #uuid "40012c1d-87cf-5109-9a23-6aca313e1115" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281014 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281014 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281014 1111 #uuid "70d61d2a-27c7-5a37-af06-55a203b16f25" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281015 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281015 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281015 1111 #uuid "012f77a9-3aa3-5515-bd22-99961b8ad332" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281016 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281016 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281016 1111 #uuid "113ab132-b40f-5ab4-be6a-6c9ba0884b3e" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281017 1034 "III only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281017 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281017 1111 #uuid "e94997a8-0a2e-5b11-84a6-6d30752c74f1" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281018 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281018 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281018 1111 #uuid "777be402-ac0f-53da-98ec-0b10ae621300" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281019 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281020 13194139534182 true] [17592186281019 1089 "q-118869" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281019 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534182 true] [17592186281019 1100 "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{y}^{2}={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281019 1113 #uuid "3c091681-9ae6-5d3e-9149-c42e1bb6941d" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281019 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281020 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281021 13194139534182 true] [17592186281020 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281022 13194139534182 true] [17592186281020 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281023 13194139534182 true] [17592186281020 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281024 13194139534182 true] [17592186281020 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281025 13194139534182 true] [17592186281020 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534182 true] [17592186281020 1041 "selection" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281020 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281021 13194139534182 true] [17592186281020 1110 #uuid "88e20f1e-782b-5788-bac7-b40b22e346c4" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281021 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281021 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281021 1111 #uuid "3401dc1c-be3b-5450-86fa-b713939750f0" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281022 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281022 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281022 1111 #uuid "d5066b58-78d8-5140-a9a4-8ca339b47771" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281023 1034 "I only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281023 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281023 1111 #uuid "7f6e971a-90bd-5d56-bdbc-dbb5935fdfc4" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281024 1034 "I, II and III" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281024 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281024 1111 #uuid "4b58bfba-b2d3-55d1-9844-ac232b42d948" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281025 1034 "II only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281025 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281025 1111 #uuid "a77cbbdd-237a-5e08-8415-610597bce1b7" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281026 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281027 13194139534182 true] [17592186281026 1089 "q-118870" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281026 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534182 true] [17592186281026 1100 "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={e}^{3-y}-2$\n- $y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+y)$" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281026 1113 #uuid "4a6041e3-4776-511a-b5c9-9dd819e86bef" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281026 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{3-y}-2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281027 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281028 13194139534182 true] [17592186281027 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281029 13194139534182 true] [17592186281027 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281030 13194139534182 true] [17592186281027 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281031 13194139534182 true] [17592186281027 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281032 13194139534182 true] [17592186281027 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534182 true] [17592186281027 1041 "selection" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281027 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281030 13194139534182 true] [17592186281027 1110 #uuid "d1ee4e6b-9bed-5358-88b0-838325134f05" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281028 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281028 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534182 true] [17592186281028 1111 #uuid "e4a44460-7381-5aef-af91-96d09f592d66" 13194139534182 true] [17592186281029 1034 "I only" 13194139534182 true] [175